Make sure the docs are up-to-date, focused, orthogonal.

If you need the human operator to visually inspect what is happening when the integration test is running (e.g. Chrome is being interacted with), set something up, interact with the command-line in some way, create a HUMAN-REQUEST.md. When response is submitted, and/or you don't need another examination, delete the file. Use this for whatever is best done by a human operator. If something needs to be executed in the cli, provide instructions for bash.

Use DEBUGGING.md to record information relevant when debugging hard problems over multiple iterations. It should facilitate exploring and keeping track of causes, solutions, expirements, what's ruled out, etc (create a pragmatic system). When debugging is done, it should be deleted.

Note, `*.include` files, TODO.md, DEBUGGING.md, HUMAN-REQUEST.md, are only part of the harness used to work on this project, they are not part of this project's runtime or distribution and deployments of this project will not have them available.

Be proactive about getting rid of unnecessary code/text and refactoring code for better reuse, better maintenance.

You must split code and documentation files into small files, so that they don't exceed 250 lines. We need focused, highly orthogonal files.

